      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A 500-person gala, you say? Well, I'm sure the guests will be buzzing around like a swarm of excited, champagne-fueled, high-society goblins, eager to mingle and make their way to the dinner. To manage the guest flow, I'd recommend a strategic approach, similar to how a seasoned goblin leader navigates a complex network of underground tunnels.\n\nFirst, let's break down the transition into manageable chunks. Divide the 500 guests into smaller groups, say 100-150 per \"wave.\" This will allow you to stagger the arrivals and prevent congestion at the entrance. You can also consider assigning a \"goblin ambassador\" – a friendly, well-dressed staff member – to greet each wave and guide them to the main ballroom.\n\nTo ensure a seamless transition, I'd suggest the following timing and staff positioning:\n\n1. **Pre-dinner cocktail hour**: Have a team of staff members stationed at the entrance, dressed in formal attire, to greet guests and direct them to the foyer. This will help to filter the crowd and prevent bottlenecks.\n2. **Wave 1**: As the first wave arrives, have the staff members escort them to the main ballroom, where they'll be",
